If a patient does not take an opioid then every clinician can determine a lowest-effective-dose with a product-labeling.
If a patient does not take an opioid and a dose-calibration is needed for the patient then every clinician can calibrate a lowest-effective-dose with a pain-severity.
If a patient does not take an opioid and the patient has a renal-insufficiency and a dose-calibration is needed for the patient then every clinician can calibrate a lowest-effective-dose with a renal-insufficiency.
If a patient does not take an opioid and the patient has a hepatic-insufficiency and a dose-calibration is needed for the patient then every clinician can calibrate a lowest-effective-dose with a hepatic-insufficiency.
If a patient does not take an opioid and the patient has an other-clinical-factor and a dose-calibration is needed for the patient then every clinician can calibrate a lowest-effective-dose with the other-clinical-factor.
